WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
We are back at the Q3 Innovation Plan.

00:00:04.094 --> 00:00:05.660
The page is no longer empty.

00:00:05.863 --> 00:00:08.935
You have chosen a priority and written down the evidence

00:00:08.935 --> 00:00:10.300
that could change your mind.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.340
But the broad idea is still sitting there: improve the member experience.

00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:20.055
Maybe your version says transform onboarding, reinvent support,

00:00:20.055 --> 00:00:22.900
or use AI to personalize everything.

00:00:23.178 --> 00:00:24.860
Those ambitions can be useful.

00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:27.300
They are hard to test as a single bet.

00:00:27.865 --> 00:00:31.268
Chapter Five of Accelerated opens with an empty planning document

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:33.828
and asks us to think big while building small.

00:00:34.223 --> 00:00:35.919
The first move is to shrink the question.

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:38.856
Instead of “What should we build for everyone?”

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.824
ask, “What is one moment we can understand better this week?”

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:46.535
In our fictional membership service, that moment is the first

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:47.901
task after someone joins.

00:00:48.363 --> 00:00:51.276
A small prototype can show us whether the instruction helps

00:00:51.276 --> 00:00:51.936
a person begin.

00:00:52.331 --> 00:00:54.368
It cannot prove the whole program works.

00:00:54.677 --> 00:00:55.307
That is fine.

00:00:55.616 --> 00:00:57.739
The point of a small test is to let reality

00:00:57.792 --> 00:01:00.341
correct the plan before the plan becomes expensive.

00:01:00.991 --> 00:01:03.241
Now picture the persona deck beside that plan.

00:01:03.508 --> 00:01:04.137
It looks finished.

00:01:04.575 --> 00:01:07.380
There is a stock photo, a name, a city,

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:10.409
a favorite app, and a line about wanting convenience.

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:14.038
In Chapter Six, I describe a gorgeous deck that did

00:01:14.038 --> 00:01:15.721
not help my team make decisions.

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:19.049
That is an uncomfortable confession because the work looked careful.

00:01:19.529 --> 00:01:22.121
The question was whether the details pointed to a real

00:01:22.121 --> 00:01:22.975
moment in the product.

00:01:23.574 --> 00:01:25.910
If we say our member is thirty-four and busy,

00:01:26.176 --> 00:01:28.598
what should the team do differently on the first task?

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.707
We do not know.

00:01:30.007 --> 00:01:32.366
If we say the member needs to understand where to

00:01:32.366 --> 00:01:35.287
start without asking for help, we can observe a situation,

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.359
compare a design, and notice what we missed.

00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:40.535
The first statement paints a person.

00:01:41.058 --> 00:01:43.138
The second names a job in context.

00:01:43.631 --> 00:01:46.905
Do not throw away demographic information when it is genuinely

00:01:46.905 --> 00:01:48.697
relevant and responsibly gathered.

00:01:48.964 --> 00:01:51.792
Just do not let a polished portrait stand in for

00:01:51.792 --> 00:01:53.049
evidence about behavior.

00:01:53.401 --> 00:01:55.823
The persona has to earn its place in the decision.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.502
The book makes this shift with Sarah.

00:01:58.502 --> 00:02:02.447
A static description tells us who she supposedly is.

00:02:02.563 --> 00:02:05.362
A job description asks what she is trying to do

00:02:05.362 --> 00:02:06.587
in a particular moment.

00:02:06.991 --> 00:02:09.777
In the book example, she is preparing for a Monday

00:02:09.777 --> 00:02:13.361
meeting and needs progress updates without sending messages that make

00:02:13.361 --> 00:02:14.703
her feel like a micromanager.

00:02:15.226 --> 00:02:17.007
That gives a team something to work with.

00:02:17.426 --> 00:02:20.104
There is a time pressure, a current workaround,

00:02:20.328 --> 00:02:21.469
and a risk to trust.

00:02:21.992 --> 00:02:24.498
The same person can have a different job on Wednesday

00:02:24.498 --> 00:02:26.162
when a blocked project needs attention.

00:02:26.665 --> 00:02:29.854
That is why a living persona follows the situation,

00:02:30.164 --> 00:02:31.006
not only the person.

00:02:31.562 --> 00:02:34.732
For our fictional service, write: “When a new member opens

00:02:34.732 --> 00:02:37.866
the first task, they need to know what action matters now,

00:02:38.261 --> 00:02:41.279
so they can begin without asking staff to translate the instructions.”

00:02:41.973 --> 00:02:43.498
That sentence is a hypothesis.

00:02:43.912 --> 00:02:45.309
We have not interviewed a member.

00:02:45.704 --> 00:02:47.998
We can still use it to decide what to test

00:02:48.094 --> 00:02:48.979
and what to ask.

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:52.477
Put a question mark beside the part you are least sure about.

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:54.568
The mark is a reminder to listen,

00:02:54.878 --> 00:02:56.062
not a flaw in the card.

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:59.536
Once the job is visible, the build can get smaller.

00:03:00.102 --> 00:03:03.035
We do not need a complete onboarding platform to learn

00:03:03.046 --> 00:03:04.571
whether the first action is clear.

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:07.540
We can make three versions of a first-task card:

00:03:07.764 --> 00:03:10.612
one with a short instruction, one with a guided step,

00:03:10.708 --> 00:03:12.660
and one that points to a person for help.

00:03:13.182 --> 00:03:15.627
Then we can ask what each version asks of the

00:03:15.627 --> 00:03:17.012
member and of the staff.

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:18.951
The book's sequence matters.

00:03:19.218 --> 00:03:20.060
Shrink the question.

00:03:20.498 --> 00:03:23.772
Generate possible approaches without falling in love with the first one.

00:03:23.996 --> 00:03:25.479
Anchor the choice to strategy.

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:28.679
Let AI help with an early draft if the inputs are safe.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:32.160
A quick draft is useful because it gives us something to inspect.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:33.825
It is not a user test.

00:03:34.259 --> 00:03:37.685
In the synthetic demonstration, I choose the guided step as

00:03:37.685 --> 00:03:40.734
the first prototype because it addresses the starting-point question

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:41.843
from our decision file.

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:43.806
I keep the other versions visible.

00:03:44.226 --> 00:03:46.860
If research shows that the instruction was already clear,

00:03:46.956 --> 00:03:48.098
I will change course.

00:03:48.535 --> 00:03:50.786
The purpose of the prototype is to put the assumption

00:03:50.839 --> 00:03:51.810
where we can see it.

00:03:52.356 --> 00:03:55.294
A living persona needs a record of where it came from.

00:03:55.609 --> 00:03:58.214
On the card, write the job, the context,

00:03:58.545 --> 00:04:03.234
the signal you observed, what remains uncertain, and the next validation question.

00:04:03.568 --> 00:04:05.274
Add a source and a review date.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.032
If this card is based on a real interview or

00:04:08.032 --> 00:04:11.658
a permitted product signal, cite the approved record without exposing

00:04:11.658 --> 00:04:12.830
the person in the course file.

00:04:13.116 --> 00:04:14.599
If it is a synthetic exercise,

00:04:15.164 --> 00:04:19.378
say so in the working evidence layer and leave “observed signal” blank.

00:04:19.772 --> 00:04:21.682
You can write a proposed signal to look for,

00:04:21.948 --> 00:04:23.346
but do not call it observed.

00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:28.233
Chapter Six is explicit that synthetic data can help generate hypotheses.

00:04:29.054 --> 00:04:34.164
It cannot replace real research or justify a high-stakes irreversible decision.

00:04:34.624 --> 00:04:37.515
This is especially easy to forget when a simulated person

00:04:37.568 --> 00:04:39.008
speaks in a convincing voice.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.441
A plausible quote is still invented.

00:04:41.665 --> 00:04:45.280
In our example, we do not know where actual members hesitate.

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:46.816
The card should say that plainly.

00:04:47.188 --> 00:04:50.590
A useful persona can hold an unknown without collapsing.

00:04:51.028 --> 00:04:54.473
That is how it stays alive long enough to become more accurate.

00:04:54.774 --> 00:04:57.665
Imagine the test happens and the team learns that members

00:04:57.718 --> 00:05:01.826
understand the first instruction but pause because they cannot tell

00:05:01.826 --> 00:05:02.998
how long the task will take.

00:05:03.393 --> 00:05:04.406
That would change the card.

00:05:04.915 --> 00:05:07.336
The job might be less about finding the next action

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:10.024
and more about knowing whether there is enough time to begin.

00:05:10.291 --> 00:05:13.181
I am making up that result to show how revision works;

00:05:13.363 --> 00:05:16.083
it is not a claim about a product or a customer.

00:05:16.588 --> 00:05:19.118
The important move is to date the update and keep

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:21.399
the old assumption visible in the decision file.

00:05:21.964 --> 00:05:24.963
A persona that changes with evidence is more useful than

00:05:24.963 --> 00:05:27.372
a deck that stays beautiful while the work moves on.

00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:31.001
This does not require collecting every click or building a

00:05:31.001 --> 00:05:32.258
profile of every person.

00:05:32.653 --> 00:05:35.074
It requires consent, an approved source,

00:05:35.256 --> 00:05:39.000
and the discipline to separate what you observed from what you inferred.

00:05:39.419 --> 00:05:41.926
Ask whether the card helps you make today's product choice.

00:05:42.278 --> 00:05:44.785
If it cannot, revise the job or the question.

00:05:45.222 --> 00:05:47.345
The person behind the work deserves that care.

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:50.246
Pause and finish one living persona card.

00:05:50.617 --> 00:05:52.312
Write the job in a specific moment,

00:05:52.579 --> 00:05:54.190
the current workaround if you know it,

00:05:54.371 --> 00:05:58.072
the observed signal or an honest blank, the biggest uncertainty,

00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:01.998
the next question, the source, and the date you will review it.

00:06:02.324 --> 00:06:04.746
Then look at all four pages of your decision file.

00:06:05.012 --> 00:06:07.775
The first page names the choice and the data boundary.

00:06:08.084 --> 00:06:10.548
The second shows the priority and the alternatives.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.450
The third names evidence that could change your mind.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.520
This page keeps the human situation in view while you

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:17.521
design a small test.

00:06:17.888 --> 00:06:20.359
That is a real working file,

00:06:20.617 --> 00:06:22.454
even if much of it is still provisional.

00:06:22.454 --> 00:06:26.134
In the next lesson, the workshop will turn more raw

00:06:26.134 --> 00:06:28.259
feedback into traceable signal.

00:06:28.758 --> 00:06:31.159
For now, hold on to the small question.

00:06:31.346 --> 00:06:33.079
Build only enough to learn.

00:06:33.471 --> 00:06:35.359
Keep the card open to correction.

00:06:35.958 --> 00:06:38.678
That is how we move faster without leaving the person behind.
